00:00:26.345 --> 00:00:28.200
And that was a live stream.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:29.279
And you may have watched it.

00:00:29.279 --> 00:00:34.185
It's available uh, here for you to,
uh, look at if you, uh, weren't able

00:00:34.185 --> 00:00:35.865
to participate in the live stream.

00:00:36.165 --> 00:00:41.595
It is also going to be available as a
lesson with subtitles, with the, uh, the

00:00:41.595 --> 00:00:47.925
transcript in our English library at LingQ
where we have a number of videos from Dr.

00:00:47.925 --> 00:00:51.735
Krashen that you can use if you're working
on improving your English and if you

00:00:51.735 --> 00:00:54.045
want to become more familiar with Dr.

00:00:54.045 --> 00:00:55.205
Krashen's ideas.

00:00:56.294 --> 00:00:58.754
Now, in the conversation with Dr.

00:00:58.754 --> 00:01:03.254
Krashen he had a, he has wonderful
stories to tell, but one of his stories

00:01:03.254 --> 00:01:08.835
was that, uh, he was in a French class
or he heard about someone who was

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:12.765
in a French class and who was always
criticized for his pronunciation.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:15.090
His pronunciation wasn't very natural.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:19.680
It wasn't, I mean, we don't have to be
totally native, but it was still very

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.160
much far away from French pronunciation.

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:28.350
He was in a way hiding behind
his English pronunciation.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:32.910
And then for whatever reason,
he decided that he was going to

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:36.630
impersonate, imitate a French person.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:42.420
So he arrived one day in front of the
class and he put on a beret because

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.790
people think French people all wear...

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:50.010
french men, all wear berets, which they
don't, and he pretended to be a Frenchman.

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:54.570
And he was, you know, we can imitate
French people speaking English, like

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:58.860
very often, some people can imitate
the French accent, the English...

00:01:59.115 --> 00:02:03.135
but when they go to speak French,
they're still using their English accent.

00:02:03.585 --> 00:02:07.665
So he decided that he was going to
pretend to be a Frenchman with his beret,

00:02:07.665 --> 00:02:09.975
with whatever other disguise he had on.

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:15.269
And he spoke French and he spoke with a
very good accent with good pronunciation.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:18.690
And the teacher said, where has
that pronunciation been hiding?

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:21.900
And of course he was in the mood for that.

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:27.515
And so I think we can achieve if not
native-like pronunciation, which is more

00:02:27.515 --> 00:02:33.245
difficult to achieve, but we can achieve
comfortable pronunciation pronunciation

00:02:33.245 --> 00:02:37.805
that the native speaker enjoys listening
to, even with an accent that feels

00:02:37.805 --> 00:02:44.855
natural, that is not strained, but is
free of the inhibitions that too often

00:02:44.855 --> 00:02:51.235
people feel when they're hanging back in
the safety of sort of pronouncing French

00:02:51.445 --> 00:02:59.700
...  in other words, staying with the less
risky sort of pronunciation of not making

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:05.160
an effort to imitate, not to take that,
that jump into the, what might be cold

00:03:05.165 --> 00:03:10.590
water of trying to actually sound like
whatever language it is we're learning.

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:12.030
I mentioned to Dr.

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:17.114
Krashen that I have referred to
this as cultural weightlessness and

00:03:17.114 --> 00:03:20.864
that this is a psychological aspect
of achieving good pronunciation.

00:03:20.924 --> 00:03:24.195
There are many other things that
we can do to improve pronunciation.

00:03:24.464 --> 00:03:28.484
Uh, listening is certainly one,
listening to things that engage us

00:03:28.484 --> 00:03:30.165
emotionally is going to help us.

00:03:30.464 --> 00:03:35.084
But also at some point you have to
recognize that you have to sort of lift

00:03:35.084 --> 00:03:37.394
yourself up, this cultural weightlessness.

00:03:37.394 --> 00:03:41.295
You're not a part of
your native culture now.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:47.220
You in fact are gonna try to sort
of immerse yourself, throw yourself

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:52.049
into the water, into the culture of
the language that you're learning.

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:54.269
You're gonna pretend to be one of them.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:59.220
And if you do that, and, and I
think that's, as I said, also to Dr.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:03.720
Krashen why very often actors, I
remember once hearing Jody Foster

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.959
speak French, she has phenomenal
pronunciation in her French.

00:04:07.019 --> 00:04:08.655
She makes mistakes on her.

00:04:08.655 --> 00:04:13.065
gender So it's not to say that her
French is perfect, but if we are

00:04:13.065 --> 00:04:17.954
concerned about pronunciation, if we
think of an actor or an actress that

00:04:17.954 --> 00:04:23.145
has to, they're no longer, she's no
longer Jody Foster, she is whatever

00:04:23.175 --> 00:04:25.094
person she's playing in the movie.

00:04:25.215 --> 00:04:29.740
And so actors are used to the
idea of projecting them themselves

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:31.330
into the role of someone else.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:35.620
And when we speak a language, not
only do we have to learn the words and

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:38.860
the phrases and so forth and improve
our comprehension, which I always

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:42.580
stress, but if we want to improve our
pronunciation, if we want to achieve

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:48.495
natural, comfortable pronunciation,
comfortable for you using it, comfortable

00:04:48.495 --> 00:04:52.995
for the person listening to it - it
needn't be perfect - I think that requires

00:04:53.145 --> 00:04:58.185
that willingness to play act to, to
sort of pretend you're someone else.

00:04:58.395 --> 00:05:04.035
And once you've done that a few
times, that then becomes the natural

00:05:04.185 --> 00:05:09.240
pronunciation for you because you have
now moved as a speaker, I use French,

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:12.960
but it could be any language, you've
moved from being sort of a foreigner

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:17.159
attempting to speak the language to
this new role where you see yourself

00:05:17.159 --> 00:05:20.219
as a member of that cultural group.

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:24.510
And I think if we can learn to do
that, and it's certainly what I try

00:05:24.515 --> 00:05:29.430
to do when I learn a language, we
then develop what I would call natural

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:33.210
pronunciation, which is not the same
as native pronunciation, because

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:39.330
realistically it's very difficult to
achieve, uh, uh, a level of pronunciation

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:43.890
that is where you'll be mistaken for a
native, by and large not gonna happen.

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:47.370
However, you can achieve a
level of pronunciation that is

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:49.980
very pleasant, very natural.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:53.520
So that's my recommendation when it
comes to pronunciation and I'll leave

00:05:53.525 --> 00:05:57.720
you with a couple of, uh, videos that
I've done in the past on pronunciation.

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:03.280
You may also wanna visit the, uh, Krashen
interview whether, uh, at the YouTube

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:06.039
channel here or in our library at LingQ.

00:06:06.219 --> 00:06:07.210
Thank you for listening.

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:08.020
Bye for now.

